  ("One person took offence and pulled a gun" 0.5258 0.8838 0.3542 0.3707 0.3707 0.0165 0.0048 0.3047 0.0356)
  ("Ya he sacado entrada" 0.1092 0.2699 0.3708 0.3871 0.3871 0.0163 0.0048 0.2225 0.0196)
  ("I’ve already got a ticket*" 0.5258 0.7321 0.3707 0.3872 0.3872 0.0165 0.0048 0.2915 0.0320)
  ("*For the various Spanish equivalents of ‘ticket’, see 44.1.5 note 1." 0.0920 0.6385 0.3994 0.4158 0.4158 0.0165 0.0048 0.2959 0.0363)
  ("(1) Un/una is usual if the object has special characteristics: llevaba (una) falda blanca ‘she was" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4281 0.4445 0.4443 0.0165 0.0073 0.2551 0.0225)
  ("wearing a white skirt’, tenía . . . una carita de chico pecoso . . . (FU, Sp.) ‘she had a cute face like" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4446 0.4610 0.4609 0.0165 0.0066 0.3078 0.0701)
  ("a freckled boy’s’. But the indefinite article does not always exclude the possibility of a generic" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4610 0.4775 0.4775 0.0165 0.0063 0.2777 0.0096)
  ("meaning. The NGLE 15.9e points out that siempre escribe sus novelas con un bolígrafo ‘(s)he always" 0.0920 0.9079 0.4775 0.4940 0.4939 0.0165 0.0051 0.3279 0.0794)
  ("writes his/her novels with a ball-point pen’ either means ‘with a certain ball-point pen’ or ‘with" 0.0920 0.9082 0.4940 0.5105 0.5105 0.0165 0.0049 0.3624 0.0529)
  ("any ball-point pen’; . . . con bolígrafo limits the meaning to ‘any ball-point pen’. Note also tengo" 0.0920 0.9080 0.5105 0.5270 0.5269 0.0165 0.0062 0.3333 0.0315)
  ("móvil desde hace años ‘I’ve had a mobile phone/cell phone (one or more) for years’, where un móvil" 0.0920 0.9080 0.5270 0.5435 0.5434 0.0165 0.0042 0.3646 0.0439)
  ("could imply one specific phone." 0.0920 0.3626 0.5435 0.5600 0.5600 0.0165 0.0048 0.3323 0.0481)
  ("(2) Use of un/una with unqualified nouns may hint at some suppressed comment: tiene un coche/" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5722 0.5887 0.5885 0.0165 0.0045 0.3667 0.0225)
  ("una casa . . . ‘you should see his car/house . . .’, tiene unos ojos . . . ‘you should see his/her eyes . . .’." 0.0920 0.9080 0.5887 0.6052 0.6051 0.0165 0.0039 0.3739 0.0300)
  ("This may sound admiring or insinuating when applied to people, e.g. marido ‘husband’, novio" 0.0919 0.9081 0.6052 0.6217 0.6216 0.0165 0.0066 0.3116 0.0368)
  ("‘boyfriend’, novia ‘girlfriend’, e.g. tiene una mujer . . . ‘he’s got a wife (and is she . . .)’." 0.0919 0.8060 0.6217 0.6382 0.6381 0.0165 0.0048 0.3639 0.0992)
  ("(3) If it would be normal to have more than one of the things denoted, or if the idea of ‘one’" 0.0919 0.9081 0.6504 0.6669 0.6667 0.0165 0.0065 0.2993 0.0227)
  ("is relevant, the indefinite article must be used: tiene mujer y un hijo (EP, Mex., dialogue) ‘he’s got" 0.0919 0.9080 0.6669 0.6834 0.6832 0.0165 0.0048 0.3609 0.0139)
  ("a wife and one child’, ¿tienes un dólar? (obviously not *¿tienes dólar?) ‘do you have a dollar?’," 0.0919 0.9081 0.6834 0.6999 0.6998 0.0165 0.0065 0.2669 0.0096)
  ("tiene un novio en Burgos y otro en Huelva ‘(s)he’s got one boyfriend in Burgos and another in" 0.0919 0.9081 0.6999 0.7164 0.7163 0.0165 0.0074 0.2304 0.0378)
  ("Huelva’." 0.0919 0.1658 0.7164 0.7329 0.7329 0.0165 nil 0.3449 0.0738)
  ("4.1.9 Retention of indefinite article before qualified nouns" 0.0920 0.7627 0.7568 0.7776 0.7776 0.0208 0.0068 0.3089 0.0541)
  ("When nouns are modified by a clause, phrase or adjective, they become specific and the indefi-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7853 0.8018 0.8018 0.0165 0.0052 0.3362 0.0512)
  ("nite article may be obligatory (brackets indicate where it is optional): tengo padre ‘I’ve got a" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8018 0.8183 0.8182 0.0165 0.0076 0.2613 0.0326)
  ("father’ but tengo un padre que es inaguantable ‘I’ve got an unbearable father’, era (un) hombre de" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8183 0.8348 0.8347 0.0165 0.0063 0.2464 0.0577)
  ("costumbres cuidadosas (AM, Mex.) ‘he was a man of prudent customs’, han organizado unas mani-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8348 0.8513 0.8512 0.0165 0.0053 0.3577 0.0879)
  ("festaciones pacíficas ‘they’ve organized peaceful demonstrations’. But if the resulting noun phrase" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8513 0.8678 0.8677 0.0165 0.0042 0.3218 0.0837)
  ("is still generic, the indefinite article may still be omitted: tú eres (un) hombre respetable ‘you’re" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8678 0.8843 0.8842 0.0165 0.0069 0.2956 0.0139)
  ("a respectable man’, es pastor protestante ‘he’s a Protestant minister’, el doctor Urdino es hombre" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8843 0.9008 0.9007 0.0165 0.0071 0.2772 0.0096)
  ("serio, además de buen gerente (El Tiempo, Col.) ‘Doctor Urdino is a serious man as well as a good" 0.0920 0.9081 0.9008 0.9173 0.9172 0.0165 0.0057 0.3333 0.0417)
  ("manager’." 0.0920 0.1793 0.9173 0.9338 0.9338 0.0165 nil 0.2948 0.0874))
