  ("   (CF, Mex. In Spain los anteojos = las gafas)" 0.1092 0.4724 0.2060 0.2225 0.2224 0.0165 0.0048 0.3612 0.0144)
  ("Ha venido sin camisa" 0.1092 0.2749 0.2226 0.2389 0.2389 0.0163 0.0048 0.3610 0.0235)
  ("un gato sin cola" 0.1092 0.2321 0.2391 0.2554 0.2554 0.0163 0.0048 0.2848 0.0214)
  ("You won’t cut it without a knife" 0.4995 0.7697 0.1730 0.1895 0.1895 0.0165 0.0048 0.3910 0.0332)
  ("Sometimes he smeared his frameless glasses" 0.4995 0.8751 0.1895 0.2060 0.2060 0.0165 0.0048 0.3653 0.0931)
  ("(S)he’s come without a shirt on" 0.4995 0.7635 0.2225 0.2390 0.2390 0.0165 0.0048 0.3735 0.0569)
  ("a cat without a tail" 0.4995 0.6571 0.2390 0.2555 0.2555 0.0165 0.0048 0.3732 0.0096)
  ("But if the idea of ‘one’ is emphasized, or, in most cases, if the noun is qualified by an adjective" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2671 0.2836 0.2836 0.0165 0.0059 0.3228 0.0297)
  ("or clause, the indefinite article is required: sin un céntimo ‘without a (single) cent’, sin un amigo a" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2836 0.3001 0.3000 0.0165 0.0053 0.3284 0.0181)
  ("quien contar sus problemas ‘without a friend to tell his problems to’." 0.0920 0.6513 0.3001 0.3166 0.3165 0.0165 0.0048 0.3471 0.0432)
  ("(d) It is not used after con when it means ‘wearing’, ‘equipped with’, and in many other adver-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3288 0.3453 0.3452 0.0165 0.0054 0.3498 0.0246)
  ("bial phrases:" 0.0919 0.1986 0.3453 0.3618 0.3618 0.0165 0.0048 0.2541 0.0315)
  ("Siempre va con abrigo" 0.1092 0.2802 0.3736 0.3898 0.3898 0.0163 0.0048 0.3000 0.0628)
  ("una casa con jardín" 0.1092 0.2602 0.3901 0.4063 0.4063 0.0163 0.0048 0.2537 0.0300)
  ("La Esfinge es un león con cabeza de hombre" 0.1092 0.4436 0.4066 0.4228 0.4228 0.0163 0.0048 0.2948 0.0193)
  ("  (JLB, Arg.)" 0.1092 0.2179 0.4229 0.4394 0.4394 0.0165 0.0041 0.3824 0.0604)
  ("Lo escribí con (un) lápiz" 0.1092 0.2964 0.4394 0.4559 0.4558 0.0163 0.0048 0.2753 0.0193)
  ("(S)he always wears an overcoat" 0.4995 0.7663 0.3734 0.3899 0.3899 0.0165 0.0048 0.3104 0.0434)
  ("a house with a garden" 0.4995 0.6877 0.3899 0.4064 0.4064 0.0165 0.0048 0.3322 0.0096)
  ("The Sphinx is a lion with a man’s head" 0.4995 0.8282 0.4064 0.4229 0.4229 0.0165 0.0048 0.3749 0.0322)
  ("I wrote it with a pencil" 0.4995 0.6918 0.4394 0.4559 0.4559 0.0165 0.0048 0.4101 0.0065)
  ("4.1.13 Omission in exclamations, after qué, and before tal, medio," 0.0920 0.8559 0.4799 0.5007 0.5007 0.0208 0.0068 0.3776 0.1893)
  ("cierto, otro, semejante" 0.1718 0.4342 0.5000 0.5208 0.5208 0.0208 0.0068 0.3517 0.0731)
  ("The indefinite article is omitted in the following types of phrase:" 0.0920 0.6380 0.5285 0.5450 0.5450 0.0165 0.0048 0.3466 0.0322)
  ("¡Extraña coincidencia!" 0.1092 0.2852 0.5568 0.5730 0.5730 0.0163 0.0048 0.2408 0.0695)
  ("¡Menudo follón!" 0.1092 0.2360 0.5733 0.5895 0.5895 0.0163 0.0048 0.3909 0.0717)
  ("¡Qué cantidad!/¡qué ruido!/¡qué pena!" 0.1092 0.4058 0.5898 0.6060 0.6060 0.0163 0.0048 0.2756 0.0396)
  ("¿Cómo ha podido hacer tal/semejante cosa/" 0.1092 0.4385 0.6063 0.6225 0.6225 0.0163 0.0048 0.2970 0.0546)
  ("   una cosa así?" 0.1092 0.2411 0.6228 0.6390 0.6390 0.0163 0.0048 0.2269 0.0589)
  ("media pinta/medio kilo" 0.1092 0.2850 0.6392 0.6555 0.6555 0.0163 0.0048 0.3651 0.0460)
  ("cierta mujer/otra cerveza" 0.1092 0.3018 0.6557 0.6720 0.6720 0.0163 0.0048 0.2716 0.0431)
  ("What a strange coincidence!" 0.4995 0.7381 0.5566 0.5731 0.5731 0.0165 0.0048 0.3334 0.0464)
  ("What a mess!" 0.4995 0.6130 0.5731 0.5896 0.5896 0.0165 0.0048 0.4455 0.0464)
  ("What a quantity/noise/pity!" 0.4995 0.7448 0.5896 0.6061 0.6061 0.0165 0.0048 0.3418 0.0464)
  ("How could (s)he have done such a thing?" 0.4995 0.8517 0.6061 0.6226 0.6226 0.0165 0.0048 0.3308 0.0426)
  ("half a pint/kilo" 0.4995 0.6304 0.6391 0.6556 0.6556 0.0165 0.0048 0.3347 0.0329)
  ("a certain woman/another beer" 0.4995 0.7602 0.6556 0.6721 0.6721 0.0165 0.0048 0.3107 0.0096)
  ("See 10.7 for cierto and 10.13 for otro." 0.0920 0.3916 0.6838 0.7002 0.7001 0.0165 0.0048 0.2762 0.0286)
  ("4.2 Unos/Unas" 0.0920 0.2986 0.7285 0.7528 0.7528 0.0243 0.0142 0.2301 0.0395)
  ("The Spanish indefinite article can be used in the plural with a variety of meanings (for a com-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7597 0.7762 0.7762 0.0165 0.0062 0.3028 0.0322)
  ("parison of algunos and unos, which may sometimes both mean ‘some’, see 10.4.2. For the pronoun" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7762 0.7927 0.7926 0.0165 0.0042 0.3834 0.0643)
  ("uno, see the Index)." 0.0920 0.2544 0.7927 0.8092 0.8091 0.0165 0.0048 0.2851 0.0348)
  ("4.2.1 Uses of unos/unas" 0.0920 0.3745 0.8333 0.8541 0.8541 0.0208 0.0068 0.2651 0.0541)
  ("(a) before numbers, ‘approximately’:" 0.0920 0.4074 0.8618 0.8783 0.8783 0.0165 0.0048 0.3335 0.0225)
  ("Costó unos treinta y cinco dólares" 0.1092 0.3708 0.8901 0.9063 0.9063 0.0163 0.0048 0.2666 0.0439)
  ("It cost about thirty-five dollars" 0.4995 0.7585 0.8899 0.9064 0.9064 0.0165 0.0048 0.2963 0.0128)
  (". . . y unos cinco minutos después se detuvo	  and about five minutes later it stopped" 0.1092 0.8491 0.9064 0.9229 0.9228 0.0165 0.0048 0.4873 0.0048)
  ("  (GGM, Col.)" 0.1092 0.2331 0.9229 0.9394 0.9393 0.0165 0.0048 0.4547 0.0781))
  (64
  ("4.2 Unos/Unas 51" 0.7643 0.9088 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0091 0.3011 0.0228)
  ("(b) before plural nouns, ‘some’ or ‘a few’, or sometimes ‘a set of’:" 0.0920 0.6484 0.0832 0.0997 0.0997 0.0165 0.0048 0.3811 0.0246)
  ("De momento no, pero si me invitas a unas" 0.1092 0.4336 0.1120 0.1283 0.1283 0.0163 0.0048 0.3729 0.0225)
  ("Not right now, but if you buy me a couple" 0.4995 0.8572 0.1119 0.1283 0.1283 0.0165 0.0048 0.3834 0.0328)
  ("  copitas a lo mejor me lo pienso	   of drinks, maybe I’ll think about it" 0.1092 0.8103 0.1284 0.1448 0.1448 0.0165 0.0048 0.9222 0.0731)
  ("  (CORPES, Arg.)" 0.1092 0.2629 0.1449 0.1613 0.1613 0.0165 0.0041 0.3546 0.1054)
  ("Todavía tenía unos restos de fe" 0.1092 0.3437 0.1615 0.1777 0.1777 0.0163 0.0048 0.2506 0.0599)
  ("(S)he still had some vestiges of faith" 0.4995 0.8049 0.1614 0.1778 0.1778 0.0165 0.0048 0.3389 0.0434)
  ("Sonreí . . . pero fue peor: unos dientes" 0.1092 0.3985 0.1780 0.1942 0.1942 0.0163 0.0048 0.2962 0.0500)
  ("I smiled, but it was worse: a set of" 0.4995 0.7876 0.1779 0.1943 0.1943 0.0165 0.0048 0.4189 0.0065)
  ("  amarillos aparecieron (CRG, Sp.)	   yellow teeth appeared" 0.1092 0.7082 0.1944 0.2108 0.2107 0.0165 0.0048 0.7800 0.0909)
  ("La compañía anunció unos resultados" 0.1092 0.4006 0.2110 0.2272 0.2272 0.0163 0.0048 0.2732 0.0193)
  ("The company announced a set of results" 0.4995 0.8413 0.2109 0.2273 0.2273 0.0165 0.0048 0.3042 0.0322)
  ("   mucho peores de lo que esperaban los	   much worse than investors expected" 0.1092 0.8297 0.2274 0.2438 0.2437 0.0165 0.0048 0.5950 0.0144)
  ("  inversores (El País, Sp.)" 0.1092 0.3162 0.2439 0.2603 0.2602 0.0165 0.0048 0.2964 0.0981)
  ("Está a unas calles de sus casas" 0.1092 0.3432 0.2605 0.2767 0.2767 0.0163 0.0048 0.2583 0.0342)
  ("It’s a few streets away from their houses" 0.4995 0.8415 0.2603 0.2768 0.2768 0.0165 0.0048 0.3692 0.0263)
  ("  (La Jornada, Mex.)" 0.1092 0.2771 0.2768 0.2933 0.2932 0.0165 0.0048 0.3568 0.0449)
  ("When used thus it may simply moderate the force of a following noun. It can therefore add a" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3055 0.3220 0.3220 0.0165 0.0065 0.3253 0.0509)
  ("modest note:" 0.0920 0.2018 0.3220 0.3385 0.3385 0.0165 0.0048 0.3329 0.0630)
  ("Mira estas fotos—son unas vistas tomadas" 0.1092 0.4392 0.3508 0.3671 0.3671 0.0163 0.0048 0.3828 0.0396)
  ("Look at these photos – they’re a couple" 0.4995 0.8306 0.3507 0.3672 0.3672 0.0165 0.0048 0.2918 0.0435)
  ("  en Guadalajara" 0.1092 0.2467 0.3673 0.3836 0.3836 0.0163 0.0048 0.2655 0.0375)
  ("  of shots taken in Guadalajara" 0.4995 0.7671 0.3672 0.3837 0.3836 0.0165 0.0048 0.3042 0.0362)
  ("Se sintió viejo, triste, inútil, y con unos" 0.1092 0.4146 0.3838 0.4001 0.4001 0.0163 0.0048 0.3017 0.0182)
  ("He felt old, sad, useless, and with an" 0.4995 0.8104 0.3837 0.4002 0.4002 0.0165 0.0048 0.3842 0.0253)
  ("  deseos de llorar tan urgentes que no pudo" 0.1092 0.4479 0.4003 0.4166 0.4166 0.0163 0.0048 0.2466 0.0674)
  ("   urge to weep that was so urgent that" 0.4995 0.8298 0.4002 0.4167 0.4167 0.0165 0.0048 0.3450 0.0144)
  ("  hablar más (GGM, Col.)	   he could speak no more" 0.1092 0.7215 0.4167 0.4332 0.4331 0.0165 0.0048 1.1041 0.0678)
  ("(c) Before nouns that appear in the plural, unos shows that only one is meant. If the noun denotes" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4454 0.4619 0.4618 0.0165 0.0040 0.3527 0.0214)
  ("symmetrical objects like trousers, binoculars, scissors, or before pairs of articles like gloves, shoes," 0.0920 0.9081 0.4619 0.4784 0.4784 0.0165 0.0038 0.3397 0.1054)
  ("unos/unas means ‘a pair of’:" 0.0920 0.3246 0.4784 0.4949 0.4948 0.0165 0.0048 0.3315 0.0806)
  ("Me caí por unas escaleras/por una escalera" 0.1092 0.4380 0.5072 0.5235 0.5235 0.0163 0.0048 0.3031 0.0257)
  ("Voy a tomarme unas vacaciones" 0.1092 0.3556 0.5237 0.5400 0.5400 0.0163 0.0048 0.3058 0.0307)
  ("unos pantalones/unas gafas/unas cortinas" 0.1092 0.4329 0.5402 0.5565 0.5565 0.0163 0.0048 0.2360 0.0375)
  ("Llevaba unas botas de ante azul (ES, Mex.)" 0.1092 0.4447 0.5566 0.5731 0.5730 0.0165 0.0048 0.3306 0.0592)
  ("I fell down some stairs" 0.4995 0.6920 0.5071 0.5236 0.5236 0.0165 0.0048 0.4035 0.0065)
  ("I’m going to have a holiday/vacation" 0.4995 0.8175 0.5236 0.5401 0.5401 0.0165 0.0048 0.3266 0.0289)
  ("a pair of trousers (US pants)/glasses/curtains" 0.4995 0.8898 0.5401 0.5566 0.5566 0.0165 0.0048 0.2850 0.0096)
  ("She was wearing a pair of blue suede boots" 0.4995 0.8667 0.5566 0.5731 0.5731 0.0165 0.0048 0.3325 0.0305)
  ("(d) Use of unos/unas may show that the plural noun following is not being used generically:" 0.0920 0.8738 0.5853 0.6018 0.6017 0.0165 0.0048 0.3363 0.0246)
  ("Son payasos" 0.1092 0.2039 0.6141 0.6304 0.6304 0.0163 0.0048 0.1864 0.0300)
  ("Son unos payasos" 0.1092 0.2462 0.6306 0.6469 0.6469 0.0163 0.0048 0.2137 0.0300)
  ("Son zorros" 0.1092 0.1918 0.6471 0.6634 0.6634 0.0163 0.0048 0.2036 0.0300)
  ("Son unos zorros" 0.1092 0.2340 0.6636 0.6799 0.6799 0.0163 0.0048 0.2262 0.0300)
  ("They’re (circus) clowns" 0.4995 0.6966 0.6140 0.6305 0.6305 0.0165 0.0048 0.3218 0.0648)
  ("They’re (acting like) clowns" 0.4995 0.7348 0.6305 0.6470 0.6470 0.0165 0.0048 0.3322 0.0648)
  ("They’re foxes (species)" 0.4995 0.6915 0.6470 0.6635 0.6635 0.0165 0.0048 0.2493 0.0648)
  ("They’re really cunning/like foxes" 0.4995 0.7839 0.6635 0.6800 0.6800 0.0165 0.0048 0.2815 0.0648)
  ("(e) Unos/unas may be needed to show that the following noun is a noun and not an adjective or" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6922 0.7086 0.7085 0.0165 0.0051 0.3271 0.0225)
  ("noun used as an adjective, as in son místicos ‘they’re mystic(al)’, son unos místicos ‘they’re mystics/" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7087 0.7251 0.7250 0.0165 0.0039 0.3709 0.0445)
  ("day-dreamers’. See 4.1.11 for examples." 0.0920 0.4252 0.7252 0.7416 0.7416 0.0165 0.0048 0.3382 0.1286)
  ("(1) Sometimes use of unos makes little difference: el pacifismo debería traducirse en unos compor­" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7538 0.7703 0.7701 0.0165 0.0067 0.2992 0.0225)
  ("tamientos políticos que no tuviesen ninguna indulgencia con los violentos (La Vanguardia, Sp., unos" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7703 0.7868 0.7867 0.0163 0.0075 0.2417 0.0760)
  ("optional) ‘pacifism ought to be translated into (a set of) patterns of political behaviour which" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7868 0.8033 0.8033 0.0165 0.0069 0.2824 0.0774)
  ("show no indulgence towards the violent’." 0.0920 0.4443 0.8033 0.8198 0.8198 0.0165 0.0048 0.3318 0.0460)
  ("(2) Unos cannot be used to answer ¿cuántos? ‘how many?’ The NGLE 20.3u notes that to the ques-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8320 0.8485 0.8483 0.0165 0.0039 0.3603 0.0225)
  ("tion ¿cuántos estudiantes había? one can reply algunos, pocos, unos cuantos, varios (‘several’) or with" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8485 0.8650 0.8649 0.0165 0.0047 0.2867 0.0336)
  ("a number, but not *unos, just as one would probably not reply ‘some’ in English." 0.0920 0.7714 0.8650 0.8815 0.8815 0.0165 0.0048 0.3582 0.0096)
  ("(3) Unos cuantos/unas cuantas may be used to mean ‘a few’, ‘couple of’: si un mesero tardaba dema-" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8937 0.9102 0.9100 0.0165 0.0048 0.3456 0.0225)
  ("siado en traernos la cuenta daba unos cuantos gritos en francés (ES, Mex., dialogue; mesero = camarero" 0.0920 0.9080 0.9102 0.9267 0.9266 0.0165 0.0051 0.3054 0.0396))
  (65
  ("52 The indefinite article" 0.0905 0.2809 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0046 0.3458 0.0248)
  ("in Spain) ‘if a waiter took too long to bring us the bill/check she uttered a couple of shouts" 0.0920 0.9081 0.0832 0.0997 0.0997 0.0165 0.0074 0.2499 0.0168)
  ("in French’." 0.0920 0.1819 0.0997 0.1162 0.1162 0.0165 0.0048 0.2896 0.0168)
  ("4.2.2 Omission of unos/unas" 0.0920 0.4272 0.1403 0.1611 0.1611 0.0208 0.0068 0.3234 0.0541)
  ("There is a widespread tendency in written Spanish, especially in newspapers, to avoid the use" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1688 0.1852 0.1852 0.0165 0.0061 0.2940 0.0487)
  ("of unos (and of algunos) in sentences of the kind expertos americanos afirman que . . . ‘American" 0.0920 0.9080 0.1853 0.2017 0.2016 0.0165 0.0070 0.2856 0.0169)
  ("experts claim that . . .’. This journalistic trick hides the fact that only a few experts were actually" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2018 0.2182 0.2182 0.0165 0.0051 0.3431 0.0620)
  ("consulted. Spoken Spanish requires los if the meaning is ‘all American experts’, algunos if the" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2182 0.2347 0.2346 0.0165 0.0070 0.2894 0.0878)
  ("meaning is ‘some’." 0.0920 0.2504 0.2347 0.2512 0.2512 0.0165 0.0048 0.4068 0.0746)
  ("In other cases, omission produces a literary effect: eléctricas letras verdes intermitentes anunciaron la" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2634 0.2799 0.2798 0.0165 0.0044 0.3078 0.0177)
  ("salida del vuelo (MVM, Sp.) ‘flashing green electric lights announced the departure of the flight’," 0.0920 0.9082 0.2799 0.2964 0.2963 0.0165 0.0056 0.3296 0.0449)
  ("where unas letras verdes would have been more usual. Also días después, una noche, luces verdes par-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2964 0.3129 0.3128 0.0165 0.0042 0.3425 0.0530)
  ("padearon en los cristales de mis balcones (JMa, Sp.) ‘one night a few days later green lights flickered" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3129 0.3294 0.3293 0.0165 0.0049 0.3283 0.0703)
  ("in my balcony windows’." 0.0920 0.3074 0.3294 0.3459 0.3459 0.0165 0.0048 0.3818 0.0168))
  (66
  ("5 Adjectives" 0.0920 0.4049 0.0777 0.1275 0.1275 0.0489 0.0417 0.2961 0.0252)
  ("The main points discussed in this chapter are:" 0.0920 0.4808 0.1846 0.2011 0.2011 0.0165 0.0048 0.3230 0.0322)
  ("• The forms of adjectives (Section 5.2)" 0.1092 0.4357 0.2133 0.2298 0.2298 0.0165 0.0048 0.3247 0.0117)
  ("• Compound adjectives like ‘light blue’, ‘socio-political’ (Sections 5.2 and 5.4)" 0.1092 0.7714 0.2298 0.2463 0.2463 0.0165 0.0048 0.3366 0.0117)
  ("• Shortened adjectives (e.g. buen for bueno) (Section 5.5)" 0.1092 0.5847 0.2463 0.2628 0.2627 0.0165 0.0048 0.2833 0.0117)
  ("• Agreement of adjectives (Section 5.6)" 0.1092 0.4434 0.2628 0.2793 0.2793 0.0165 0.0048 0.3277 0.0117)
  ("• Adjectives of place (e.g. americano, madrileño) (Section 5.7)" 0.1092 0.6175 0.2793 0.2958 0.2957 0.0165 0.0048 0.3308 0.0117)
  ("• The suffix -ísimo (Section 5.8)" 0.1092 0.3763 0.2958 0.3123 0.3123 0.0165 0.0048 0.3266 0.0117)
  ("• The position of adjectives (Section 5.10)" 0.1092 0.4651 0.3123 0.3288 0.3288 0.0165 0.0048 0.2929 0.0117)
  ("• ‘Relational’ adjectives like industria hotelera ‘hotel industry’ (see Glossary) (Section 5.11)" 0.1092 0.8712 0.3288 0.3453 0.3452 0.0165 0.0048 0.2987 0.0117)
  ("5.1 General remarks about Spanish adjectives" 0.0920 0.7166 0.3736 0.3978 0.3978 0.0243 0.0079 0.2972 0.0395)
  ("(a) Nearly all Spanish adjectives agree with nouns and pronouns in number, and many also" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4048 0.4213 0.4213 0.0165 0.0075 0.2764 0.0225)
  ("agree in gender. They therefore either have two forms, e.g. natural/naturales, or four, e.g. bueno/" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4213 0.4378 0.4377 0.0165 0.0059 0.2977 0.0461)
  ("buena/buenos/buenas. A few, e.g. macho ‘male’, violeta ‘violet’, are invariable in form." 0.0920 0.7893 0.4378 0.4543 0.4542 0.0165 0.0048 0.3635 0.1703)
  ("(b) The position of adjectives is a subtle question, the difference between un problema difícil and" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4665 0.4830 0.4829 0.0165 0.0053 0.3074 0.0246)
  ("un difícil problema ‘a difficult problem’ being virtually untranslatable in English." 0.0920 0.7653 0.4830 0.4995 0.4994 0.0165 0.0048 0.3397 0.0214)
  ("(c) It is necessary to distinguish ‘descriptive’ adjectives (adjetivos calificativos), e.g. ‘a big book’," 0.0920 0.9081 0.5117 0.5281 0.5281 0.0165 0.0063 0.2634 0.0214)
  ("‘a blond girl’ from ‘relational’ adjectives (adjetivos relacionales), e.g. ‘a nuclear power-station’, ‘a" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5282 0.5446 0.5446 0.0165 0.0070 0.2910 0.0150)
  ("pedestrian crossing’. See Glossary for definitions." 0.0920 0.5009 0.5447 0.5611 0.5611 0.0165 0.0048 0.2884 0.0803)
  ("(d) Many, but not all, Spanish adjectives become nouns if a determiner (see Glossary) is added:" 0.0920 0.9079 0.5734 0.5898 0.5898 0.0165 0.0053 0.3323 0.0246)
  ("joven/estas jóvenes ‘young’/‘these young women’; see 5.9b. Nouns can also occasionally be used" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5898 0.6063 0.6062 0.0165 0.0056 0.3010 0.0849)
  ("like adjectives, as in ella es más mujer que Julia ‘she’s more (of a) woman than Julia’ (or ‘more femi-" 0.0920 0.9082 0.6063 0.6228 0.6227 0.0165 0.0044 0.4071 0.0311)
  ("nine’); see 5.9a." 0.0920 0.2206 0.6228 0.6393 0.6393 0.0165 0.0048 0.3029 0.0538)
  ("However, adjectives are formed in unpredictable ways from nouns, e.g. automóvil – automovilístico," 0.0920 0.9080 0.6515 0.6680 0.6679 0.0165 0.0039 0.3823 0.0829)
  ("metal – metálico, leche ‘milk’ lechal, lechoso and lácteo." 0.0920 0.5253 0.6680 0.6845 0.6844 0.0163 0.0048 0.3548 0.0428)
  ("(e) Some adjectives can be used with object pronouns and the verb ser: me es fácil ‘it’s easy" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6967 0.7132 0.7131 0.0165 0.0077 0.2680 0.0227)
  ("for me’, nos son imprescindibles ‘they’re indispensable to us’; but most cannot. See 14.6.3 for" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7132 0.7297 0.7296 0.0165 0.0080 0.2819 0.0247)
  ("discussion." 0.0920 0.1871 0.7297 0.7462 0.7462 0.0165 nil 0.2400 0.0951)
  ("(f) Adjectival participles ending in -ante, -iente, e.g. vinculante ‘binding’, preocupante ‘worrying’," 0.0920 0.9081 0.7584 0.7749 0.7748 0.0165 0.0056 0.3108 0.0203)
  ("are discussed under participles at 23.6." 0.0920 0.4205 0.7749 0.7914 0.7914 0.0165 0.0048 0.2825 0.0261)
  ("(g) The gerund in -ndo is a verb form and must not be used as an adjective: una muñeca que anda" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8036 0.8201 0.8200 0.0165 0.0050 0.3230 0.0235)
  ("or una muñeca andante ‘a walking doll’, not una muñeca andando ‘a doll walking’. For two excep-" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8201 0.8366 0.8365 0.0165 0.0055 0.3221 0.0181)
  ("tions to this rule, see 5.3. For a discussion of the Gerund see Chapter 24." 0.0920 0.7008 0.8366 0.8531 0.8531 0.0165 0.0048 0.3197 0.0418)
  ("(h) Spanish adverbs are invariable in form, even when they look like adjectives: los teléfonos" 0.0920 0.9082 0.8653 0.8818 0.8817 0.0165 0.0077 0.2662 0.0246)
  ("están fatal ‘the phones are in a dreadful state’, estamos mejor ‘we’re feeling better’. See 35.3.3. for" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8818 0.8983 0.8982 0.0165 0.0056 0.3077 0.0407)
  ("discussion." 0.0920 0.1862 0.8983 0.9148 0.9148 0.0165 nil 0.2425 0.0942))
  (67
  ("54 Adjectives" 0.0905 0.2008 0.0492 0.0683 0.0679 0.0154 0.0061 0.3321 0.0248)
  ("5.2 Forms of adjectives" 0.0920 0.4090 0.0821 0.1063 0.1063 0.0243 0.0079 0.3260 0.0395)
  ("There are three types of Spanish adjectives:" 0.0920 0.4580 0.1133 0.1298 0.1298 0.0165 0.0048 0.2771 0.0487)
  ("• Type 1 adjectives agree in number and gender with the noun or pronoun (5.2.1)" 0.1092 0.8045 0.1412 0.1577 0.1577 0.0165 0.0048 0.3302 0.0629)
  ("• Type 2 adjectives agree in number but not gender (5.2.2)" 0.1092 0.6067 0.1577 0.1742 0.1742 0.0165 0.0048 0.3301 0.0629)
  ("• Type 3 adjectives do not change their form: they are not numerous (5.2.3 and 5.2.4)" 0.1092 0.8307 0.1742 0.1907 0.1907 0.0165 0.0048 0.3418 0.0629)
  ("5.2.1 Type 1 adjectives (agree in number and gender)" 0.0920 0.7080 0.2148 0.2356 0.2356 0.0208 0.0068 0.2946 0.0541)
  ("These include adjectives that end with: -o, -án, -és, -ín, -ón, -or (with the exceptions like inferior" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2433 0.2598 0.2597 0.0165 0.0063 0.3057 0.0496)
  ("listed below), -ote and -ete." 0.0920 0.3153 0.2598 0.2763 0.2762 0.0165 0.0048 0.3478 0.0467)
  ("For macho, modelo, oro see 5.2.3–4; for cortés, descortés, montés, afín and marrón see 5.2.2." 0.0920 0.8155 0.2885 0.3050 0.3049 0.0165 0.0048 0.3211 0.0288)
  ("How to form the feminine singular of type 1 adjectives:" 0.0920 0.5616 0.3165 0.3330 0.3330 0.0165 0.0048 0.3776 0.0426)
  ("Masculine singularFeminine singular" 0.3731 0.7779 0.3512 0.3670 0.3670 0.0157 0.0041 1.8292 0.0791)
  ("ends with a vowelbuenobuena ‘good’" 0.1011 0.7325 0.3730 0.3894 0.3887 0.0164 0.0041 2.3816 0.0355)
  ("ends with a consonanthabladorhabladora ‘talkative’" 0.1011 0.7837 0.3952 0.4117 0.4109 0.0164 0.0041 2.3892 0.0355)
  ("Masculine pluralFeminine plural" 0.3731 0.7607 0.4369 0.4526 0.4526 0.0157 0.0041 2.0142 0.0791)
  ("ends with a vowelbuenosbuenas" 0.1011 0.6927 0.4586 0.4743 0.4743 0.0157 0.0041 2.2762 0.0355)
  ("ends with a consonanthabladoreshabladoras" 0.1011 0.7181 0.4803 0.4961 0.4960 0.0157 0.0041 2.2653 0.0355)
  ("Important: in writing, a final -z is replaced by c before e. Any accent on the final vowel of the" 0.0920 0.9080 0.5096 0.5261 0.5259 0.0165 0.0064 0.3149 0.0936)
  ("masculine singular disappears, as in the cases of inglés, musulmán, pillín in the following chart:" 0.0920 0.8907 0.5261 0.5426 0.5425 0.0165 0.0048 0.3478 0.0866)
